          "t": "p",
          "text": "The third paper is the smallest and, oddly, the most immediately practical. A Johns Hopkins group ran eight 45-minute sessions of ordinary individual cognitive behavioural therapy — nothing tailored to menopause, standard skills like behavioural activation and reframing — with perimenopausal and postmenopausal women reporting mood symptoms. Twenty started, sixteen finished all eight. Depression scores on the PHQ-9 fell 42.1% (8.74 to 5.06, P=0.001) and anxiety scores on the GAD-7 fell 56.2% (6.98 to 3.06, P=0.004)."
