      "faq": [
        {
          "q": "Should I get a bone scan before starting a GLP-1?",
          "a": "It is what Kohrt recommends, and the reasoning is straightforward: bone loss is silent, so without a baseline reading there is nothing to compare a later scan against, and the first sign of a problem may be a broken wrist or hip. The complication is that a DXA scan before 65 may not be routinely offered or funded where you are — the US screening guideline starts at 65, and Kohrt’s own point is that this may be too late given how many women in their fifties are now on these drugs. So this is a request to make of your doctor with a reason attached, rather than something you can assume will be arranged for you. It is a more reasonable ask if you have other risk factors: a family history of fracture, early menopause, low body weight, long-term steroid use, or a previous fracture as an adult."
        },
        {
          "q": "Does taking HRT protect my bones if I’m losing weight on a GLP-1?",
          "a": "Oestrogen therapy does help maintain bone density during menopause — that part is well established, and it is the mechanism behind why bone loss accelerates when oestrogen falls in the first place. What has not been established is whether it offsets the bone cost of significant weight loss on top of that, which is a different question and one nobody has run the trial for. There is a related finding worth knowing about but not over-reading — hormone therapy users [lost about 35% more weight on tirzepatide](/blog/tirzepatide-hrt-weight-loss-menopause-2026/) in an observational study this year — and losing more weight is not obviously the outcome you want for your skeleton, either. Hormone therapy is worth discussing on its own merits with a doctor who knows your history; treating it as bone insurance against a GLP-1 is going further than the evidence does."
        }
      ]
