          "q": "If the mechanism only works in mice, is the study worth anything to me?",
          "a": "It is worth something, but not what the headline suggests. What the study genuinely establishes is causation in an animal: the researchers didn’t just observe inflamed guts alongside a low-fibre diet, they moved the vesicles into other mice and watched the inflammation follow. That tells you the gut lining actively signals to the immune system based on what it is being fed, rather than simply degrading. The specific molecule doing the signalling — miR-6240 — has no human equivalent, so nobody can yet say which molecule plays that role in us, or whether the effect is as strong. Treat it as a mechanism worth investigating in humans, not as a reason to change anything you’re doing."
