      "faq": [
        {
          "q": "Will I be paid for taking part?",
          "a": "Usually not in the sense of a wage. UK academic studies normally reimburse travel and sometimes offer a modest payment for your time, particularly where visits are long or involve procedures like biopsies; commercial drug trials pay more often. The registry record rarely states it clearly, so ask the study team directly — it is a completely standard question and asking it will not affect whether you are accepted."
        },
        {
          "q": "Can I take part if I live in Ukraine, or outside the UK?",
          "a": "Only in studies with a site you can actually get to. Trials are run at named hospitals and clinics, and travel to another country is not funded, so a UK-only study is realistically out of reach from Kyiv. The exception is the large international drug trials — the two elecoglipron studies in this list recruit at Ukrainian and British sites simultaneously. If you are in Ukraine, search ClinicalTrials.gov with Ukraine as the location filter; there are fewer studies, but they are mostly the big multinational ones."
        },
        {
          "q": "I’m on HRT. Does that rule me out?",
          "a": "Of some studies, yes. Several trials of non-hormonal approaches specifically exclude women currently using hormone therapy, and one in this list asks for a year without it — because they cannot tell whether an effect came from their supplement or your patches. That is scientifically reasonable and practically annoying. What you should not do is come off HRT to qualify. Talk to whoever prescribes it, and look for a study whose criteria you already fit."
        },
        {
          "q": "Is it safe, and what if I get the placebo?",
          "a": "Every study here has been through an ethics committee, which is what the registry record and its approval numbers represent. That does not mean zero risk: drug trials carry the side effects of the drug and are monitored accordingly, while the supplement and probiotic studies carry very little. You may well be in the placebo group, and in a double-blind study nobody involved knows who is until it ends. You can withdraw at any point, for any reason, without it affecting your normal care — that is not a courtesy, it is a condition of the study being allowed to run."
        },
        {
          "q": "Do I need a referral from my GP?",
          "a": "Almost never. Most of these studies recruit directly and you can contact the team yourself using the details on the registry page. Many will then ask your permission to write to your GP, and it is worth saying yes so your medical record reflects what you are actually taking or wearing. If a study needs your clinical data — HbA1c, scans, prescriptions — they will ask you to consent to that separately."
        }
      ]
